.\" tvmail(1) man page.  man(7) macros, because we are old school.
.TH TVMAIL 1 "September 2026" "tvmail 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tvmail \- a Turbo Vision mail client for a local mailbox
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tvmail
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tvmail
reads, writes and files electronic mail from a text terminal, wearing the
face of Borland's
.B Turbo Vision
\(em dropdown menus, dialog boxes with drop shadows, a cyan desktop, and
.B F10
for the menu bar.
It is, unapologetically, a DOS\-era user interface built in the current decade
to read a mailbox that most days contains three messages.
.PP
It is the presentation layer only.
Every operation that touches a mail format \(em parsing mbox files, decoding
MIME, composing, sending, deleting \(em is performed by a small companion
program,
.BR tvmail\-backend (1),
which
.B tvmail
runs as a child process.
The intent is a thin, legible front end and a back end that is useful on its
own.
.PP
.B tvmail
is a friendlier face on the very same mailbox that
.BR mail (1)
uses; it is not a walled garden.
See
.B "mailrc INTEROPERABILITY"
below.
.SH THE THREE PANES
The window is divided into a folder list on the left, a message list at the
top right, and the body of the highlighted message below it.
.TP
.B Tab ", " Shift\-Tab
Move focus between the three panes.
.TP
.BR "Up" "/" "Down" ", " "PgUp" "/" "PgDn"
Move within the focused pane.
.PP
Moving the highlight in the folder pane reloads the message list.
Moving it in the message list loads the body pane.
.B Enter
in the message list moves focus to the body so it can be scrolled.
.SH FOLDERS
.TP
.B inbox
.I /var/mail/$USER
(or
.IR $MAIL ).
Where the local MTA and
.B pop\-pull
deliver.
.TP
.B drafts
.IR $folder/drafts ,
default
.IR ~/Mail/drafts .
Messages you chose to keep but not send.
.BR "mail \-f +drafts"
opens the same file.
.TP
.B saved
.IR ~/mbox .
Where
.BR mail (1)
files messages once read.
.TP
.B trash
.IR ~/.local/share/tvmail/trash.mbox .
.B Ctrl\-D
moves a message here.
Deleting a message while
.I in
the trash folder deletes it for real.
.TP
.B dead.letter
.I $DEAD
(default
.IR ~/dead.letter ).
A single message that
.BR mail (1)
or
.B tvmail
left behind after an aborted composition.
Shown as a one\-message pseudo\-folder.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B F1
This help, but inside the program.
.TP
.B F3
Pull remote mail (runs
.BR pop\-pull ).
.TP
.B F5
Reload the current folder.
.TP
.B "Ctrl\-R" ", " "Ctrl\-N"
Reply to the selected message; compose a new one.
.TP
.B F2
Send the compose window you are currently in.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-D
Delete the selected message (to the trash folder).
.TP
.B F4
Open the address book.
.TP
.B "F6" ", " "Shift\-F6"
Cycle to the next / previous window.
.TP
.B F10
Enter the menu bar.
.TP
.B Alt\-X
Quit.
.PP
In any editor (the compose body, the signature file):
.B Shift\-Del
cut,
.B Ctrl\-Ins
copy,
.B Shift\-Ins
paste; the
.B Edit
menu carries Find, Replace and Find\-again.
.SH COMPOSING
A new message opens with the contents of
.I ~/.signature
appended (after the quoted text, on replies).
.B "Message \(-> Insert signature"
and
.B "Message \(-> Insert dead.letter"
add those by hand \(em the spiritual descendants of the
.B ~a
and
.B ~d
tilde escapes from
.BR mail (1).
.PP
Closing a compose window that has unsent, modified text offers
.BR "Save draft" ,
.BR Discard ,
or
.BR Cancel .
Saving appends the message to the
.B drafts
folder.
Selecting a message in the
.B drafts
folder and pressing
.B Enter
re\-opens it for editing; sending it then removes it from
.BR drafts .
.SH ADDRESS BOOK
.B "Message \(-> Address book"
.RB ( F4 )
lists the
.B alias
and
.B group
entries found in
.I /etc/mailrc
and
.IR ~/.mailrc ,
expanded recursively.
.B Enter
drops the expanded addresses into the
.B To:
field of your most recent compose window.
You may also simply type an alias name into
.BR To: ;
.BR tvmail\-backend (1)
expands it at send time, exactly as
.B "mail team"
would.
.SH SIGNATURE
.B "File \(-> Edit signature"
opens
.I ~/.signature
in a full editor window.
.B Ctrl\-S
writes the file; closing an unsaved change prompts first.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.mailrc ", " /etc/mailrc
Read for
.BR "set folder" ,
.BR "set DEAD" ,
and the
.BR alias / group
address book.
.TP
.I ~/.signature
Appended to new messages; editable from within the program.
.TP
.I ~/dead.letter
Honoured as the
.B dead.letter
folder and by
.BR "File \(-> Resume dead.letter" .
.TP
.I ~/Mail/drafts
The drafts mailbox (path follows
.BR "set folder" ).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tvmail/trash.mbox
The trash mailbox.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MAIL
Overrides the inbox path.
.TP
.B DEAD
Overrides
.IR ~/dead.letter .
.TP
.B MAILRC
Overrides
.IR ~/.mailrc .
.TP
.B PATH
Must contain
.BR tvmail\-backend .
.SH "mailrc INTEROPERABILITY"
.B tvmail
deliberately shares state with
.BR mail (1):
the same
.IR ~/.mailrc ,
the same address book, drafts that
.B "mail \-f +drafts"
can open, an honoured
.IR ~/dead.letter .
If you have used
.BR mailx (1)
or GNU Mailutils on this account, your aliases already work here.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.B tvmail
exits
.B 0
on a clean quit.
Anything else means Turbo Vision could not open the terminal, which is between
you and your terminal.
.SH BUGS
It is a Turbo Vision application, written in 2026, to read local mail.
Whether this constitutes a bug is left as an exercise for the reader.
.PP
Report the other kind at wherever you got this.
.SH HISTORY
The author spent roughly two decades not finding a terminal mail client he
liked, tried and abandoned several of his own, and eventually built this one.
The unabridged confession is in
.IR HISTORY.md .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR tvmail\-backend (1),
.BR mail (1),
.BR mailx (1),
.BR sendmail (8),
.BR exim (8),
.BR mbox (5)
.PP
and, for the road not taken,
.BR mutt (1)
and
.BR alpine (1).
